    "result_text": "The change is complete and the full test suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI added address normalization to the owner-creation pipeline, following the codebase's existing convention of a single-rule helper class (`OwnerEmails`, `OwnerTelephones`, `OwnerHouseholds`) paired with a small `Normalize*` step.\n\n**New files:**\n- `OwnerAddresses.java` \u2014 the single normalization rule: trim, collapse whitespace to a single space, upper-case, and expand `ST\u2192STREET`, `RD\u2192ROAD`, `AVE\u2192AVENUE` (whole-word, so `STAR` stays intact). Blank/null normalizes to `\"\"`.\n- `NormalizeOwnerAddress.java` \u2014 the pipeline's new first step. It performs the single `@RequestBody` read, normalizes the address, and publishes the request for later steps.\n\n**Changed files:**\n- `ValidateRequiredOwnerFields.java` \u2014 no longer binds the body (that responsibility moved to `NormalizeOwnerAddress`); now reads the published request via `@Val`. Because normalization runs first, its required-field check now rejects an address that is blank *after* normalization.\n- `owners.POST.yml` \u2014 wired `normalizeAddress` as the entry step before `validate`, under the same `transaction` governance.\n\n**Why this satisfies the spec:**\n- The normalized address is stored (via `BuildOwner`/mapper) and returned \u2014 verified by the whitespace/case and abbreviation tests.\n- The required-field check sees the normalized value \u2192 blank-after-normalization is a 400.\n- Household duplicate detection (`RejectDuplicateOwnerHousehold` reads `request.getAddress()`) and the shared household id (`AssignOwnerHousehold` reads `owner.getAddress()`) both now operate on the normalized address, since the request carries the normalized form from the first step onward. No logic was duplicated \u2014 the single `OwnerAddresses.normalize` is the one source of truth.\n\nFull suite: `Tests run: 3, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 BUILD SUCCESS.",
